  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="Sampling">
      <data key="d0">Sampling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of measuring the amplitude of a signal at discrete, equally spaced points in time.&lt;SEP&gt;Sampling is the process of measuring the magnitude of a signal at discrete time points, converting a continuous-time signal into a discrete-time signal.&lt;SEP&gt;Sampling is a method for converting a continuous-time analog signal into a discrete-time signal, as introduced on Page 3 and further detailed on subsequent pages.&lt;SEP&gt;Sampling is the method or process described in the image caption for converting a continuous-time analog signal into a discrete-time signal.&lt;SEP&gt;Sampling is the process of taking discrete measurements of a continuous analog signal, which precedes the quantization step formalized by Equation 1.3.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-442ee34c5a776cfa12564adbd3f829b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-713b2bab6050e468d17f2fd671a62d07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2846e3e2abff26edef2b72a265a32e6</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Discrete-Time Signal">
      <data key="d0">Discrete-Time Signal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A discrete-time signal is a signal defined only at specific sampling instants, no longer a continuous function of time.&lt;SEP&gt;A Discrete-Time Signal is a type of signal referenced in the image caption as the result obtained from sampling the continuous-time analog signal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2846e3e2abff26edef2b72a265a32e6</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442038</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Analog Signal">
      <data key="d0">Analog Signal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A signal whose amplitude is continuous and analogous to the physical quantity it represents, processed by analog circuits.&lt;SEP&gt;A signal (v_A) that varies between 0V and +15V, represented by a digital word.&lt;SEP&gt;An analog signal is a signal where the amplitude at each sampling point can take any value within a continuous range.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19dab0b0d0f5732d8dae08612cd92992&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Quantization">
      <data key="d0">Quantization</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Quantization is the process of representing each sampled value with a finite set of numbers, making the signal amplitude discrete.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantization is a method that converts a discrete-time analog signal into a digital signal represented by a sequence of numbers, expected to be discussed on Page 4.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantization is the process of mapping a continuous amplitude to a finite set of discrete levels, formalized by the binary expansion in Equation 1.3.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantization is a key concept tested in Exercise 1.7, relating to the process of mapping analog signals to digital values.&lt;SEP&gt;A process discussed in the context of analog-to-digital conversion, mentioned as part of the foundational content preceding page 5.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a13baf3efaa8ad719f72772dae8ca462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87bb565956f8e2b856ee07e0f7f38e94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-442ee34c5a776cfa12564adbd3f829b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-713b2bab6050e468d17f2fd671a62d07</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Signal">
      <data key="d0">Digital Signal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A signal represented by a sequence of numbers, each indicating the signal's amplitude at a specific sampling time.&lt;SEP&gt;A digital signal is a series of numbers representing the magnitudes of sampled values from a continuous signal after quantization.&lt;SEP&gt;A Digital Signal is a signal represented by a sequence of numbers, resulting from the analog-to-digital conversion process detailed in the document.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-442ee34c5a776cfa12564adbd3f829b8</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Binary Number System">
      <data key="d0">Binary Number System</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The binary number system uses digits 0 and 1, producing simple digital signals and circuits with two voltage levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Binary Digit (Bit)">
      <data key="d0">Binary Digit (Bit)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A binary digit (bit) is a digit in the binary system that can have a value of 0 or 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Least Significant Bit (LSB)">
      <data key="d0">Least Significant Bit (LSB)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Least Significant Bit (LSB) is the bit with the smallest place value in a binary number.&lt;SEP&gt;The least significant bit (LSB) is the bit in the binary representation with the smallest weight, corresponding to b_0 in Equation 1.3.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-713b2bab6050e468d17f2fd671a62d07</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442037</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Most Significant Bit (MSB)">
      <data key="d0">Most Significant Bit (MSB)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Most Significant Bit (MSB) is the bit with the largest place value in a binary number.&lt;SEP&gt;The most significant bit (MSB) is the bit in the binary representation with the largest weight, corresponding to b_{N-1} in Equation 1.3.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-713b2bab6050e468d17f2fd671a62d07</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442037</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Quantization Error">
      <data key="d0">Quantization Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Quantization error is the discrepancy between the original analog sample value and its digital representation.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantization error is a concept related to the process of converting analog signals to digital form, discussed on the preceding page.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantization error is the difference between the original analog value and its quantized digital representation, which is reduced by increasing the number of bits N.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantization error is the associated error from finite digital representation, which is calculated in sub-question (c) of Exercise 1.7.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a13baf3efaa8ad719f72772dae8ca462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54adab7d992eb1e107a550b8bcd3fa85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-713b2bab6050e468d17f2fd671a62d07</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Analog-To-Digital Converter (ADC)">
      <data key="d0">Analog-To-Digital Converter (ADC)</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC) is a circuit module that receives an analog input signal and outputs its corresponding N-bit digital representation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digital-To-Analog Converter (DAC)">
      <data key="d0">Digital-To-Analog Converter (DAC)</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A Digital-to-Analog Converter (DAC) is the dual circuit of an ADC, converting digital signals back to analog form.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Circuit">
      <data key="d0">Digital Circuit</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A digital circuit is an electronic circuit designed to process binary digital signals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Logic Circuit">
      <data key="d0">Logic Circuit</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A fundamental building block of digital systems, mentioned alongside storage circuits.&lt;SEP&gt;A logic circuit is a fundamental building block of digital systems that performs logical operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19dab0b0d0f5732d8dae08612cd92992</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Memory Circuit">
      <data key="d0">Memory Circuit</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A memory circuit is a fundamental building block of digital systems used for storing information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Logic Inverter">
      <data key="d0">Digital Logic Inverter</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The most basic digital circuit, introduced in Section 1.7.&lt;SEP&gt;A digital logic inverter is the most basic digital circuit, performing the logical NOT operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19dab0b0d0f5732d8dae08612cd92992</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Analog Circuit">
      <data key="d0">Analog Circuit</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of circuit that can best implement certain signal processing functions.&lt;SEP&gt;An analog circuit is an electronic circuit that processes continuous analog signals.&lt;SEP&gt;Electronic circuits designed to process analog signals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19dab0b0d0f5732d8dae08612cd92992&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 1.7 (a)">
      <data key="d0">Figure 1.7 (a)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 1.7 (a) is an illustration of a continuous-time signal before sampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 1.7 (b)">
      <data key="d0">Figure 1.7 (b)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 1.7 (b) is an illustration of a signal after sampling, showing its discrete-time nature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 1.8">
      <data key="d0">Figure 1.8</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 1.8 is an illustration showing a digital signal as a pulse train over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 1.9">
      <data key="d0">Figure 1.9</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 1.9 is a block diagram illustrating an Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Equation (1.3)">
      <data key="d0">Equation (1.3)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Equation (1.3) is the mathematical formula for representing an analog sample value as an N-bit binary number.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation (1.3) is a formula for binary encoding, specifically D = Σ (b_i * 2^i), which is required to solve the tasks in Exercise 1.7.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a13baf3efaa8ad719f72772dae8ca462</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
