      <data key="d0">Digital Watch</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A digital watch is an example of a device that uses digital signal processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Calculator">
      <data key="d0">Digital Calculator</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A digital calculator is an example of a device that uses digital signal processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Audio System">
      <data key="d0">Digital Audio System</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A digital audio system is an example of a device that uses digital signal processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Television">
      <data key="d0">Digital Television</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Digital television is an example of a system that uses digital signal processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Telephone Communication System">
      <data key="d0">Telephone Communication System</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Telephone communication systems are traditional analog systems that have been largely digitized.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Computer">
      <data key="d0">Digital Computer</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The most important digital system, built from logic circuits and storage circuits.&lt;SEP&gt;The digital computer is the most important digital system, processing digital signals representing instructions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19dab0b0d0f5732d8dae08612cd92992</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Signal">
      <data key="d0">Signal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Information that is processed, amplified, and must be preserved without distortion.&lt;SEP&gt;A time-varying quantity that can be represented graphically, whose information content is conveyed by amplitude variations over time, contained in the "swings" of its waveform.&lt;SEP&gt;A time-varying quantity, such as voltage, that carries information and can be represented in either the time domain or the frequency domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19dab0b0d0f5732d8dae08612cd92992&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1db0dd174df6afbb65ba3881d5b3205b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Signal Waveform">
      <data key="d0">Signal Waveform</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The graphical representation of a signal's amplitude over time, which is often difficult to describe mathematically but is crucial for designing signal processing circuits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Signal Processing Circuit">
      <data key="d0">Signal Processing Circuit</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A circuit designed to perform specific functions on a particular signal, requiring a good description of the signal waveform for its design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Signal Source Representation">
      <data key="d0">Signal Source Representation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Equivalent circuit models for a signal source, such as the voltage source and current source representations shown in Figure 1.1 (a) and (b).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Open-Circuit Voltage">
      <data key="d0">Open-Circuit Voltage</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The voltage observed at the output terminals of a signal source when no load is connected, denoted as ν_oc.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Short-Circuit Current">
      <data key="d0">Short-Circuit Current</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The current that flows when the output terminals of a signal source are shorted together, denoted as i_sc.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Source Resistance">
      <data key="d0">Source Resistance</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The internal resistance of a signal source, denoted as R_s, which relates the source voltage ν_s and source current i_s.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Signal Spectrum">
      <data key="d0">Signal Spectrum</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A highly useful description of a signal or any time function, representing it as a sum of sinusoidal components of different frequencies and magnitudes.&lt;SEP&gt;Signal Spectrum is the title of Section 1.2 and a key concept introduced under Chapter 1, relating to frequency domain analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The signal spectrum is the graphical representation of the frequency components of an arbitrary waveform, as depicted in the image.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-905f3d2f135d774def7cdb42035893ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2355f4759811dfd4f5be72baace3411d</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442037</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fourier Series">
      <data key="d0">Fourier Series</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A mathematical tool used to represent a periodic time function as an infinite sum of sinusoids whose frequencies are harmonically related.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier Series is a mathematical concept introduced for analyzing signal spectra, specifically mentioned in the context of a square wave example.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier Series is a mathematical tool referenced in the main text of the textbook.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier series is a mathematical method for decomposing complex periodic signals into sums of sinusoidal components.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical method for representing a periodic function as a sum of sinusoidal components (sine and cosine waves) with different frequencies and amplitudes.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier series are a method of signal analysis defined within the instructional material on Page 0 and Page 2.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier series is a component of Fourier analysis mentioned in the technical discussions under Chapter 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1db0dd174df6afbb65ba3881d5b3205b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1878e4e15958dba975a53e75f38c1afb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467c3767922cb6472da43fb7f5a7e1b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2355f4759811dfd4f5be72baace3411d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2551c96f45909c03241cd8648ab77005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abf71e9be0152596ec77f617bc3a5594</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fourier Transform">
      <data key="d0">Fourier Transform</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A mathematical method used to analyze signals by transforming them from the time domain to the frequency domain, revealing their frequency spectrum.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical tool used to obtain the spectrum of a signal, applicable to both periodic and arbitrary time functions, providing a continuous frequency representation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier Transform is a mathematical concept mentioned as a potential new topic for continuation in Section 1.2 on Page 2.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier transform is a mathematical method for representing non-periodic signals in the frequency domain.&lt;SEP&gt;A related mathematical method for representing aperiodic signals in the frequency domain, mentioned in contrast to the Fourier series used for periodic signals.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier transform is a component of Fourier analysis mentioned in the technical discussions under Chapter 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1db0dd174df6afbb65ba3881d5b3205b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1878e4e15958dba975a53e75f38c1afb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2355f4759811dfd4f5be72baace3411d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abf71e9be0152596ec77f617bc3a5594</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442037</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sinusoidal Signal">
      <data key="d0">Sinusoidal Signal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A signal of the form V_a sin(ωt), characterized by its peak amplitude V_a, angular frequency ω, and phase, crucial for circuit analysis, design, and testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Sinusoidal signal is a key concept introduced under the scope of Chapter 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2355f4759811dfd4f5be72baace3411d</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442037</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Peak Amplitude">
      <data key="d0">Peak Amplitude</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The maximum voltage or current value of a sinusoidal signal, denoted as V_a.&lt;SEP&gt;Peak amplitude, denoted by $V_a$, is the maximum voltage value in volts of a sinusoidal signal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1878e4e15958dba975a53e75f38c1afb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442037</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Angular Frequency">
      <data key="d0">Angular Frequency</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The rate of oscillation of a sinusoidal signal in radians per second, denoted as ω, where ω = 2πf.&lt;SEP&gt;Angular frequency, denoted by $\omega$, is the rate of change of the phase angle in radians per second, related to frequency $f$ by $\omega = 2\pi f$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1878e4e15958dba975a53e75f38c1afb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442037</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Frequency">
      <data key="d0">Frequency</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The number of cycles per second of a periodic signal, measured in Hertz (Hz), denoted as f, where f = 1/T.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Period">
      <data key="d0">Period</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The duration of one complete cycle of a periodic signal, measured in seconds, denoted as T.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Phase">
      <data key="d0">Phase</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The initial angle of a sinusoidal signal at a reference time, determining its horizontal shift on a time axis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Root Mean Square (rms) Value">
      <data key="d0">Root Mean Square (rms) Value</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A measure of the effective magnitude of a sinusoidal signal, equal to its peak amplitude divided by the square root of 2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Symmetrical Square Wave">
      <data key="d0">Symmetrical Square Wave</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A periodic signal, such as the one shown in Figure 1.4, that can be represented by a Fourier series as an infinite sum of sinusoids at harmonic frequencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fundamental Frequency">
      <data key="d0">Fundamental Frequency</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The lowest frequency component in the Fourier series representation of a periodic signal, denoted as ω_0, where ω_0 = 2π/T.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Harmonic">
      <data key="d0">Harmonic</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A sinusoidal component in a Fourier series whose frequency is an integer multiple of the fundamental frequency.&lt;SEP&gt;A sinusoidal component of a periodic signal with a frequency that is an integer multiple of the fundamental frequency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db51dd6aa7f66f035b774eb00b1181eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1db0dd174df6afbb65ba3881d5b3205b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
