    <node id="Exercise 1.7">
      <data key="d0">Exercise 1.7</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">An exercise involving the representation of an analog signal using a 4-bit digital word.&lt;SEP&gt;Exercise 1.7 is a pedagogical exercise located at the end of Section 1.3 on Page 5.&lt;SEP&gt;This is a detailed exercise on digital word representation mentioned as an example of the practical content within the textbook framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Exercise 1.7 is a structured, enumerated list of three sub-questions that form a pedagogical exercise on analog-to-digital conversion concepts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19dab0b0d0f5732d8dae08612cd92992&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a13baf3efaa8ad719f72772dae8ca462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1743404717606908306d8938851e7fa7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fee9a50cff994f9381f5cb09c9bccc53</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Waveform">
      <data key="d0">Waveform</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The shape of a signal; an amplifier must output a waveform that is a precise, scaled copy of the input waveform.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19dab0b0d0f5732d8dae08612cd92992</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Amplifier Output Signal">
      <data key="d0">Amplifier Output Signal</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The result of amplification, which should be an exact replica of the input signal but with a larger amplitude.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19dab0b0d0f5732d8dae08612cd92992</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Periodic Signal">
      <data key="d0">Periodic Signal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A signal that repeats its pattern over a fixed time interval, characterized by a discrete frequency spectrum including a fundamental frequency and its harmonics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Periodic Signal">
      <data key="d0">Non-Periodic Signal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A signal that does not repeat over time, characterized by a continuous frequency spectrum containing all possible frequencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Spectrum">
      <data key="d0">Spectrum</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A representation of a signal in the frequency domain, showing the distribution of its amplitude or power across different frequencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Audio Band">
      <data key="d0">Audio Band</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The frequency range from approximately 20 Hz to 20 kHz, which encompasses the spectrum of sound signals like speech and music that are audible to the human ear.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Analog Video Signal">
      <data key="d0">Analog Video Signal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A signal with a frequency spectrum typically ranging from 0 MHz to 4.5 MHz.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Time-Domain Representation">
      <data key="d0">Time-Domain Representation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A method of representing a signal by its amplitude as it varies over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Time-domain representation describes a signal as a function of time, such as $v_a(t)$.&lt;SEP&gt;A way of describing a signal by its amplitude as a function of time, showing the waveform's shape and behavior over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Time-domain representation is a method for representing signals, discussed as part of the technical content under Chapter 1.&lt;SEP&gt;Time-domain representation is a foundational concept for signal analysis discussed in the material preceding Page 3.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1db0dd174df6afbb65ba3881d5b3205b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1878e4e15958dba975a53e75f38c1afb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2355f4759811dfd4f5be72baace3411d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2551c96f45909c03241cd8648ab77005</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Frequency-Domain Representation">
      <data key="d0">Frequency-Domain Representation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A method of representing a signal by its frequency spectrum, such as V_a(ω) for a voltage signal v_a(t).&lt;SEP&gt;Frequency-domain representation describes a signal in terms of its frequency components, such as $V_a(\omega)$.&lt;SEP&gt;A way of describing a signal by its constituent sinusoidal frequencies and their amplitudes, showing the signal's spectrum.&lt;SEP&gt;Frequency-domain representation is a method for representing signals, discussed as part of the technical content under Chapter 1.&lt;SEP&gt;Frequency-domain representation is a foundational concept for signal analysis discussed in the material preceding Page 3.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1db0dd174df6afbb65ba3881d5b3205b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1878e4e15958dba975a53e75f38c1afb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2355f4759811dfd4f5be72baace3411d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2551c96f45909c03241cd8648ab77005</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sinusoidal Wave">
      <data key="d0">Sinusoidal Wave</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A periodic wave characterized by its frequency (f) and angular frequency (ω), with examples given for calculating period (T) from frequency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="UHF Television Broadcast Band">
      <data key="d0">UHF Television Broadcast Band</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A frequency band from 470 MHz to 806 MHz, with each channel allocated 6 MHz, allowing for the calculation of the number of available channels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Square Wave">
      <data key="d0">Square Wave</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A periodic waveform described by a Fourier series, used in exercises to calculate power distribution among its harmonic components.&lt;SEP&gt;Square Wave is a type of signal used as an example in the discussion of frequency spectra and Fourier series analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A square wave is an example of a complex periodic signal that can be decomposed into a sum of sinusoids via Fourier series.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1878e4e15958dba975a53e75f38c1afb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abf71e9be0152596ec77f617bc3a5594</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442037</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 1.7">
      <data key="d0">Figure 1.7</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure illustrating the process of sampling an analog voltage signal to convert it into a digital signal.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 1.7 is a potential figure that may appear on Page 3 or later in the document.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 1.7 is a visual aid referenced on Page 3, illustrating the sampling process for converting analog signals to discrete-time signals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-442ee34c5a776cfa12564adbd3f829b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2551c96f45909c03241cd8648ab77005</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Angular Frequency ω">
      <data key="d0">Angular Frequency ω</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The rate of change of phase of a sinusoidal waveform, measured in radians per second (rad/s), used as the horizontal coordinate in spectral plots.&lt;SEP&gt;The angular frequency of the signal, calculated as ω = 2πf rad/s.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff84b12278674a0234b8f4aeaf7eb163</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Harmonic Frequencies">
      <data key="d0">Harmonic Frequencies</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Frequencies that are integer multiples of a fundamental frequency (ω0), present in the spectrum of a periodic signal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fundamental Frequency ω0">
      <data key="d0">Fundamental Frequency ω0</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The lowest frequency component of a periodic signal, from which harmonic frequencies are derived.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Voltage Signal v_a(t)">
      <data key="d0">Voltage Signal v_a(t)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">An analog voltage signal used as an example, represented in both the time domain (as a waveform) and the frequency domain (as V_a(ω)).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="V_a(ω)">
      <data key="d0">V_a(ω)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The frequency-domain representation (spectrum) of the voltage signal v_a(t).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Power P">
      <data key="d0">Power P</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The rate of energy dissipation, calculated for a square wave signal applied to a resistor using either direct integration or summation of harmonic contributions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Resistor">
      <data key="d0">Resistor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An electrical component to which a square wave signal is applied for power loss calculations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sampling Times t0, t1, t2...">
      <data key="d0">Sampling Times t0, t1, t2...</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Discrete, equally spaced points in time at which the amplitude of an analog signal is measured during the sampling process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-181e1935bacdbe115a741761e8a72851</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Signal Processing">
      <data key="d0">Digital Signal Processing</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfb17561da02687c6161ff7a64fd758b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a8f977d1024cb072125fb79a1a17c1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-713b2bab6050e468d17f2fd671a62d07</data>
      <data key="d2">A digital watch is a practical application that implements digital signal processing technology.&lt;SEP&gt;Digital signal processing is the domain involving the analysis and manipulation of discrete signals, within which the binary conversion and quantization operate.&lt;SEP&gt;The domain of processing discrete-time digital signals, which contrasts with the continuous-time analog processing.</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770442035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
