    <node id="Prerequisite Reassurance Footnote for Fourier Analysis (page_footnote)">
      <data key="d0">Prerequisite Reassurance Footnote for Fourier Analysis (page_footnote)</data>
      <data key="d1">page_footnote</data>
      <data key="d2">This footnote addresses readers unfamiliar with Fourier series and transforms, referenced in the main text's discussion of signal spectra. It reassures them that these tools are not required until Chapter 6, but that a general grasp of the current section's concepts is useful for understanding earlier material. It serves to manage cognitive load and maintain reading flow in the textbook's introductory chapter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467c3767922cb6472da43fb7f5a7e1b3</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sinusoidal Voltage Signal Equation (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Sinusoidal Voltage Signal Equation (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines a sinusoidal voltage signal with peak amplitude $V_a$ and angular frequency $\omega$. It is the fundamental periodic function used as the basis for Fourier analysis, allowing complex signals to be represented as sums of sinusoids. In the context, it illustrates basic AC signal properties, connects to RMS values, and serves as the building block for understanding both time-domain and frequency-domain signal representations in electronic circuit analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1878e4e15958dba975a53e75f38c1afb</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="image_77f0495e89e86972a3ab7a0c29c9815f">
      <data key="d0">image_77f0495e89e86972a3ab7a0c29c9815f</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">Image content: {'type': 'image', 'img_path': 'C:\\RAG-Anything\\output\\RAG_test1\\hybrid_auto\\images\\27fee27753a</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff84b12278674a0234b8f4aeaf7eb163</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fourier Series Expansion of a Symmetric Square Wave (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Fourier Series Expansion of a Symmetric Square Wave (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation represents a symmetric square wave voltage signal as an infinite sum of odd-harmonic sine waves with amplitudes inversely proportional to harmonic order. It exemplifies Fourier series decomposition, demonstrating how periodic signals can be represented in the frequency domain for analysis and design of signal processing circuits. The decreasing harmonic amplitudes allow practical approximations by series truncation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db0dd174df6afbb65ba3881d5b3205b</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="image_52188ee671eaf4df20372353766f2757">
      <data key="d0">image_52188ee671eaf4df20372353766f2757</data>
