    <node id="Exercise 1.7: Application Problems on 4-bit Binary Representation of Analog Signals (list)">
      <data key="d0">Exercise 1.7: Application Problems on 4-bit Binary Representation of Analog Signals (list)</data>
      <data key="d1">list</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a three-part exercise list applying the theory of analog-to-digital conversion. It tasks the reader with: (a) converting specific analog voltages to 4-bit binary codes, (b) determining the analog voltage change corresponding to a toggle in each bit position (LSB to MSB), and (c) finding the digital representation for a fractional voltage and calculating the quantization error. Its purpose is to reinforce the preceding lesson on quantization, binary weighting, and ADC resolution within the chapter's foundational discussion on signal types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a13baf3efaa8ad719f72772dae8ca462</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ideal Linear Amplifier Equation (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Ideal Linear Amplifier Equation (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation v_o(t) = A v_i(t) models an ideal linear amplifier's input-output relationship, where the output voltage is an exact scaled replica of the input voltage. It represents fundamental analog signal amplification without distortion, contrasting with the digital signal representation discussed earlier. The equation appears in the context of explaining how weak transducer signals are amplified for reliable processing in electronic systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a8f977d1024cb072125fb79a1a17c1a</data>
      <data key="d4">RAG_test1.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1770441798</data>
    </node>
